The method startElement in GenListModuleReader has some pretty dicey code that assumes that specific attributes will be present and throws a null pointer exception if they are not. 

The specific bug is that if a specialization accidently fails to declare the domain attribute, processing will fail. This will also happen if there is no class attribute, although that is a less likely error simply because stuff won't work at all if there is no class attribute, but it will otherwise work if there is no domains attribute.

Here is my quick rework of that method to catch these situations and provide useful feedback on the problems found:

public void startElement(String uri, String localName, String qName,
Attributes atts) throws SAXException {

String attrValue = atts.getValue(Constants.ATTRIBUTE_NAME_CLASS);

 if (attrValue == null) {
 System.err.println(" - Warning, no 'class' attribute for element \"" + localName + "\".");
 }

 // WEK: FIXME: This check will return a false positive on " mytopic/topictitle " because
 // the constant string used for the match doesn't include the bounding
 // space characters.
 if (attrValue != null && attrValue.indexOf(Constants.ATTR_CLASS_VALUE_TOPIC) != -1){
String domains = atts.getValue(Constants.ATTRIBUTE_NAME_DOMAINS);
if (domains == null) {
System.err.println(" - Warning, no 'domains' attribute for element \"" + localName + "\".");
} else {
 props = StringUtils.getExtProps(domains);
 } 
 }

if (insideExcludedElement) {
++excludedLevel;
return;
}

// Ignore element that has been filtered out.
if (FilterUtils.needExclude(atts, props)) {
insideExcludedElement = true;
++excludedLevel;
return;
}

