The `InitializeProjectTask `should safely handle concurrent access to the `ServiceLoader<StoreBuilder>`. All threads should consistently see the full list of available StoreBuilder providers, and the getStore() method should always return a valid `Store `instance for supported store types (e.g., "memory").
Under concurrent access, the `ServiceLoader<StoreBuilder>` in `InitializeProjectTask `is not thread-safe. This leads to a race condition during lazy provider discovery, resulting in some threads seeing an empty or incomplete provider list. Once corrupted, the `ServiceLoader `cache remains broken for the lifetime of the JVM/ClassLoader, causing all subsequent calls to `getStore()` to fail with:
private static final Map<String, StoreBuilder> storeBuilders;

static {
 Map<String, StoreBuilder> builders = new HashMap<>();
 for (StoreBuilder sb : ServiceLoader.load(StoreBuilder.class)) {
 builders.put(sb.getType(), sb);
 }
 storeBuilders = Map.copyOf(builders);
}

private Store getStore(XMLUtils xmlUtils) {
 StoreBuilder builder = storeBuilders.get(storeType);
 if (builder == null) {
 throw new BuildException(String.format("Unsupported store type %s", storeType));
 }
 return builder.setTempDir(tempDir).setXmlUtils(xmlUtils).build();
}
Note: If `StoreBuilder` instances are not thread-safe `getStore()` should create a fresh builder instance per call.
